day34.push(mk('discharge_ob_0700', 'progressNotes', 'Progress Note', 'Obstetric and Maternal-Fetal Medicine Discharge-Readiness Note', 'B. Montenegro, MD and L. Aldrich, MD', T('Thu', '07:00'),
 '28 weeks 5 days, discharge planned about 0900: well-grown singleton, Category II tracing resolved to Category I within about 9 hours of treatment with no cesarean; contractions Day 2 benign (cervical length 3.8 cm, fibronectin negative, no steroids); BPP 8/8; today NST reactive; glucose 88.',
 'Priya is ready for discharge home today at 28 weeks 5 days with a viable, well-grown singleton. Her triage Category II tracing (baseline 165, minimal variability, recurrent late decelerations) improved with maternal correction of volume, acid-base and potassium and reached Category I at about 1100 on Day 1, avoiding an unnecessary and risky preterm cesarean. The Day 2 evening contractions were benign uterine irritability (cervical length 3.8 cm, closed; fetal fibronectin negative), so betamethasone and its risk of steroid-induced recurrent DKA were avoided. The biophysical profile on Day 3 was 8/8 and the 0630 non-stress test this morning is reactive. Labs at 0700: fasting glucose 88, sodium 136, potassium 4.2, chloride 104, bicarbonate 21, creatinine 0.52, anion gap 11, BHB 0.1, phosphorus 3.6, magnesium 2.0; urine ketones negative; hemoglobin 10.8 (expected physiologic dilution of pregnancy). Rh positive: no immune globulin. GBS testing at 36 weeks. Education completed: daily kick counts, preterm labor and preeclampsia warning signs, sick-day return criteria; teach-back by both without error. Both IVs to be removed with intact sites before she leaves. Follow-up Monday 01/04 at 0900 (MFM, NST, fluid check) and 1030 (endocrinology).', ['0630', '0900', '0650', '1100', '1030']));
day34.push(mk('endo_discharge_0700', 'progressNotes', 'Consult Note', 'Endocrinology Discharge Note', A.endo, T('Thu', '07:00'),
 'DKA in pregnancy resolved on Day 1 (glucose 242, pH 7.24, bicarbonate 10, BHB 5.4 at presentation); one fasting low on the full basal dose; home regimen glargine 20 units, lispro 1:10 plus scale; ketone meter and glucagon in hand.',
 'Priya was admitted with DKA at a glucose of only 242 mg/dL (pH 7.24, bicarbonate 10, anion gap 26, BHB 5.4) from gastroenteritis, a halved basal dose and omitted mealtime insulin. Treatment: crystalloid, regular insulin 0.1 unit/kg/h with dextrose from a glucose under 200, potassium and magnesium replacement through peripheral IVs within the 10 mEq/h limit, a 2 hour 20 minute insulin hold for a potassium of 3.2 with dextrose kept, and biochemical resolution on the 1850 draw of Day 1 (pH 7.42, bicarbonate 19, anion gap 12, BHB 0.5), with a 2-hour overlap with glargine. One fasting low (62) on Day 2 led to glargine 20 units. Since then fasting 91-92 and 88 and 1-hour postprandial values 124-134, with BHB 0.1.\n\n**Discharge regimen:** glargine 20 units at 2100; lispro 1 unit per 10 g carbohydrate 10-15 minutes before meals (about 6, 7 and 8 units) plus a correction of +1 for 121-150, +2 for 151-180, +3 for 181-210; aspirin 81 mg daily; prenatal vitamin; ondansetron 4 mg ODT every 8 hours as needed; nasal glucagon 3 mg; blood ketone meter; continuous glucose monitor. Pregnancy targets: fasting under 95, 1 hour under 140, 2 hours under 120, bedtime 90-120. I expect to raise doses with the third trimester. Clinic Monday at 1030.', ['1850', '2100', '1030']));
